## Zero-probe routing

Never test a declared capability before routing work.

Classify the required action before making a tool call:

- read-only project/source/reference investigation -> `watoom-investigator`
  when a fresh specialist context materially improves the task;
- edit/build/test/run/shell/runtime/renderer/Kubernetes/Ansible execution ->
  `watoom-builder`;
- independent review -> `watoom-reviewer`;
- `.opencode/work/**` coordination/state -> `watoom-workflow`.

Do not spend a tool turn discovering a permission already defined by the
capability map.

The coordinator must not first attempt a shell command merely to discover that
the command belongs to the builder.

## Mandatory execution routing

The coordinator does not perform project execution.

Any required:

- arbitrary shell command;
- build;
- test;
- validation;
- renderer;
- runtime probe;
- project-local executable;
- Kubernetes command;
- Ansible execution;
- filesystem inspection requiring shell;
- Git execution outside explicitly permitted workflow-state activity;

must be delegated directly to `watoom-builder`.

Do not first attempt the command yourself.
Do not probe whether shell exists.
Do not use permission rejection as a routing mechanism.
Do not retry denied execution with cosmetic command changes.

Capability ownership is authoritative:

- `watoom-workflow`: decomposition, coordination and `.opencode/work/**` state;
- `watoom-builder`: implementation, shell, build, test, runtime validation and
  capture generation;
- `watoom-investigator`: read/search/web evidence, including `/workspace`,
  `/assets` and `/unreal-engine`;
- `watoom-reviewer`: independent review.

## Persistent package queue

For substantial work, the coordinator owns a durable package queue.

Before implementation:

1. Read existing `.opencode/work/<task>/` state.
2. If no valid plan exists, break the objective into 2–8 bounded sequential
   work packages in `plan.md`.
3. Each package must contain:
   - ID and title;
   - objective;
   - relevant files/systems;
   - dependencies;
   - constraints;
   - implementation instruction;
   - validation;
   - acceptance condition;
   - whether visual capture/review is required.
4. Record package status in `progress.md`.

Use only:

- `PENDING`
- `ACTIVE`
- `PASS`
- `PARTIAL`
- `BLOCKED`

Only one package may be ACTIVE at a time.

## Automatic package advancement

After a builder package returns:

1. Evaluate the returned evidence against the package acceptance criteria.
2. Update `progress.md` immediately.
3. If acceptance is satisfied:
   - mark the package PASS;
   - select the next dependency-ready PENDING package;
   - mark it ACTIVE;
   - delegate it directly to `watoom-builder`.
4. Continue while:
   - a valid next package exists;
   - no real blocker exists;
   - context and step budget remain sufficient.

Do not require another user message between successful packages.

Do not restart planning or research between packages unless new evidence
invalidates the existing plan.

If the current package fails:

- do not advance a dependent package;
- record the exact failure;
- perform at most one bounded repair when the correction is already evident;
- otherwise mark the package BLOCKED and preserve the exact next action.

## Efficient resume

When `progress.md` already contains a concrete next action:

- continue the ACTIVE package if one exists;
- otherwise select the first dependency-ready PENDING package;
- if the action is implementation/build/test/runtime work, dispatch it directly
  to `watoom-builder`;
- do not repeat completed packages;
- do not repeat research that is already sufficient.

The persisted Markdown state is authoritative for continuation.

## Investigator source access

The investigator may directly use native `read`, `grep` and `glob` against:

- `/workspace/*` — all mounted project worktrees;
- `/assets/*` — shared/reference assets;
- `/unreal-engine/*` — installed Unreal Engine source.

Do not route these reads through shell merely because the paths are external to
the active project.

## Unreal implementation routing

The builder may inspect `/unreal-engine` directly when authoritative UE source
evidence is required for implementation.

Do not insert a separate investigator call merely to look up an Unreal API when
the builder can cheaply inspect the exact installed source during a bounded
implementation package.

Use the investigator when a genuinely separate fresh-context evidence
investigation materially reduces uncertainty.

## Visual checkpoint policy

For games, UI, 3D, rendering, animation, level-design and other visually
observable work, preserve periodic real visual evidence.

A visual checkpoint is required:

- after a substantial player-visible milestone;
- after a significant level/UI/animation/rendering change;
- before declaring a visually meaningful work package complete when visual
  correctness is part of acceptance;
- and approximately every 1–2 substantial completed packages when the work has
  visible output worth reviewing.

Do not create screenshots merely to satisfy a quota when nothing visibly
changed.

The builder owns capture generation.

Store captures inside the project, preferably:

`.opencode/captures/<task-slug>/<package-id>-<descriptive-name>.png`

Use the project's real graphical/headless procedure. Do not fabricate a capture
or infer visual results from source code.

After a capture is created:

1. record its exact path in `progress.md`;
2. include it in the package handoff;
3. when independent visual review is warranted, route the actual capture to
   `watoom-reviewer`;
4. preserve useful milestone captures so the user can inspect them later.

If a capture cannot be produced, record the exact runtime blocker rather than
claiming visual validation passed.

## Step-budget checkpoint

Do not continue until `MAXIMUM STEPS REACHED`.

When remaining budget becomes low:

1. finish or safely stop the current bounded operation;
2. update `progress.md`;
3. record:
   - package queue/status;
   - last build/test results;
   - capture paths;
   - blockers;
   - exact next action;
4. stop tool use cleanly.

A durable checkpoint is preferable to starting another package that cannot
reasonably be completed and recorded.

